6 6 0 0 1-6 6m8-9.31V4h-4.69L12 .69 8.69 4H4v4.69L.69 12 4 15.31V20h4.69L12 23.31 15.31 20H20v-4.69L23.31 12 20 8.69Z\">\n154\t          \n155\t        \n156\t      \n157\t    \n158\t  \n159\tAbstract\n160\tI Introduction\n161\tII Related Work\n162\tII-A Symbolic Sequence Modeling and Token Representation\n163\tII-B Fingering Ambiguity and Playability\n164\tII-C Constrained Decoding for Structured Generation\n165\tIII Methodology\n166\tIII-1 Decoder Token Representation\n167\tIII-2 Architecture\n168\tIII-3 Training objective\n169\tIII-4 Pitch-Validity Constrained Decoding\n170\tIV Experiment\n171\tIV-A Dataset\n172\tIV-B Evaluation Metrics\n173\tIV-C Results\n174\tIV-D Error Analysis\n175\tIV-E Pitch-Validity Constrained Decoding\n176\tV Conclusion\n177\tReferences\n178\t  \n179\t    License: CC BY 4.0\n180\t  \n181\t  \n182\tarXiv:2607.26440v1 [cs.SD] 29 Jul 2026\n183\tExplicit Note-Event Tokenization and Pitch-Validity Constrained Decoding for MIDI-to-Tablature Transcription\n184\tAuthor Name\n185\t  \n186\t1st Ting-Kai Hsu\n187\tGraduate Institute of Communication Engineering\n188\tNational Taiwan University\n189\tTaipei, Taiwan\n190\tr13942132@ntu.edu.tw\n191\t2nd Wei-Chin Wang\n192\tDepartment of Electrical Engineering\n193\tNational Taiwan University\n194\tTaipei, Taiwan\n195\tb10502010@ntu.edu.tw\n196\t3rd Kai-Xi Hong\n197\tDepartment of Electrical Engineering\n198\tNational Taiwan University\n199\tTaipei, Taiwan\n200\tb10401006@ntu.edu.tw\n201\t4th Yu-Hua Chen\n202\tGraduate Institute of Networking and Multimedia\n203\tNational Taiwan University\n204\tTaipei, Taiwan\n205\tf08946011@ntu.edu.tw\n206\tAbstract\n207\tGuitar tablature transcription predicts the string and fret position for each note so that the resulting tablature reproduces the target musical part. Prior sequence-to-sequence approaches have shown promising results on large-scale datasets, but their generalization behavior across different dataset scales remains less explored. In this work, we propose a guitar tablature transcription framework with explicit note-event tokenization and regularized training. The proposed decoder token representation incorporates note-event tokens together with TAB tokens, allowing note boundaries, pitch-related events, and string–fret positions to be represented more explicitly.\n208\tWe evaluate the proposed framework on DadaGP, a large-scale dataset, and François Leduc, a small-scale dataset. Our method improves tablature accuracy over the Fretting Transformer baseline on DadaGP, with especially strong gains when trained directly on the small-scale Leduc dataset. We further introduce a pitch-validity constrained decoding strategy that masks pitch-invalid TAB candidates during generation rather than correcting them after decoding and simultaneously preserves the original timing and note structure from the input. This constraint improves tablature accuracy and provides a controlled setting for measuring how much error remains after pitch-invalid predictions are removed.\n209\tOur code will be released at: https://github.com/MusicGuitarTab/GuitarTab\n210\tI Introduction\n211\tGuitar tablature (TAB) is a form of music notation that explicitly specifies the string and fret positions for each note on the guitar fretboard.\n212\tBy contrast, Musical Instrument Digital Interface (MIDI) is widely used as a symbolic music representation that encodes musical events, such as pitch, timing, and control information [21], but it does not specify the physical playing position of each note on the guitar.\n213\tTherefore, symbolic guitar tablature\n214\ttranscription is not merely a pitch transcription task. Instead, it is an inference problem in which each pitch event must be mapped to one of\n215\tseveral possible string–fret realizations. Classical approaches have addressed this ambiguity using explicit optimization methods, such as dynamic programming and genetic algorithms [17, 20, 14].\n216\tRecent transformer-based approaches formulate guitar tablature transcription as a sequence modeling problem, learning the mapping from MIDI-like event representations to TAB tokens [5, 4].\n217\tThese methods have demonstrated promising results on large-scale tablature datasets, such as DadaGP [16]. However, two important aspects remain insufficiently explored.\n218\tFirst, the role of target-side tokenization in tablature generation has received limited attention. In autoregressive formulations such as the Fretting Transformer [5], the decoder output mainly consists of TIME_SHIFT_TICKS and TAB&lt;string,fret&gt; tokens. This representation explicitly encodes the physical string–fret position, but note boundaries and pitch-related events are only implicit in the generated sequence.\n219\tSecond, prior work often relies on large-scale datasets, synthetic data, or combined-dataset training, leaving the effect of dataset scale on model generalization less clear.\n220\tMotivated by these observations, we propose an explicit note-event tokenization for guitar tablature transcription from MIDI-like event representations.\n221\tInstead of generating only timing and TAB tokens, our decoder generates a structured sequence containing\n222\tNOTE_ON_PITCH, TAB&lt;string,fret&gt;, and NOTE_OFF_PITCH tokens and TIME_SHIFT_TICKS.\n223\tThe NOTE_ON_PITCH and NOTE_OFF_PITCH tokens expose note boundaries and pitch-related information on the target side, while the TAB&lt;string,fret&gt; token specifies the physical realization of each note.\n224\tThis design aligns the target-side representation more closely with the MIDI-like input event structure and separates note-event modeling from string–fret assignment.\n225\tIn addtion to decoder token representation, we introduce a pitch-validity constrained decoding strategy for guitar tablature generation.\n226\tSince the input MIDI-like event sequence provides the pitch of each target note, the decoder can restrict TAB candidates to string–fret positions that physically realize the corresponding pitch.\n227\tThis constraint prevents pitch-invalid TAB tokens from being selected during decoding and provides a diagnostic setting for separating pitch consistency from the remaining ambiguity of choosing among multiple pitch-valid string–fret positions.\n228\tWe evaluate the proposed framework under two dataset-scale regimes: DadaGP [16], a large-scale tablature dataset, and the François Leduc dataset [15], a small-scale dataset.\n229\tFor fair comparison, we retrain the Fretting Transformer separately on each dataset and compare it with our method under the same dataset-specific setting.\n230\tIn our experiments, the baseline can be trained successfully on DadaGP but tends to overfit on the François Leduc dataset, whereas our full framework achieves better pitch and tablature accuracy in both settings.\n231\tIn addition to standard transcription metrics, we conduct note-level error analysis and pitch-validity constrained decoding experiments to better understand the remaining challenges of guitar tablature transcription. In the constrained setting, pitch-invalid tablature candidates are removed during decoding using the target pitch, while the timing and note structure are preserved from the input. This analysis estimates the upper-bound performance when pitch-level errors are controlled, and also demonstrates the improvement of token and tablature accuracy after applying pitch-validity constrained decoding in both models.\n232\tOur contributions are summarized as follows:\n233\t• \n234\tWe propose an explicit note-event decoder token representation for guitar\n235\ttablature transcription from MIDI-like event representations. The proposed tokenization exposes note boundaries and pitch-related events on the target side while preserving string–fret tablature information.\n236\t• \n237\tExperimental results show that our full framework improves pitch and tablature accuracy over the retrained Fretting Transformer baseline on DadaGP dataset.\n238\tFurther analysis shows that pitch-valid string–fret ambiguity remains a major source of error.\n239\t• \n240\tWe introduce a pitch-validity constrained decoding strategy for guitar tablature generation. By masking string–fret candidates that are inconsistent with the source MIDI pitch, the proposed decoding strategy reduces pitch-invalid TAB predictions and provides a diagnostic setting for analyzing string–fret ambiguity.\n241\tII Related Work\n242\tII-A Symbolic Sequence Modeling and Token Representation\n243\tSymbolic music modeling commonly represents musical content as discrete token sequences, where the design of event tokens determines how temporal, pitch, and structural information is exposed to the model.\n244\tSelf-attention-based models, such as Music Transformer and its variants, have demonstrated strong ability to capture long-range musical structure from symbolic token sequences [9, 10, 19, 8, 3].\n245\tFor guitar tablature transcription, token representation is also critical because it determines how musical timing, pitch-related note events, and the corresponding string and fret information are encoded in the sequence.\n246\tThis representation affects how explicitly the model can learn the relationship between pitch events and their possible playing positions.\n247\tRecent studies have adopted transformer-based models for symbolic guitar tablature transcription.\n248\tMIDI-to-Tab [4] uses a BART-based [11] encoder–decoder Transformer with a masked language modeling objective, where string tokens are masked and predicted from MIDI-derived symbolic tokens.\n249\tSince pitch information is given in the input, the predicted string can be used to derive the corresponding fret, making the task closer to sequence labeling.\n250\tFretting Transformer [5] formulates guitar tablature as an autoregressive sequence-to-sequence generation problem, where the decoder generates TAB tokens.\n251\tHowever, the decoder output format in the Fretting Transformer consists of TIME_SHIFT_TICKS and TAB&lt;string,fret&gt; tokens, which provides limited explicit note-event information during generation.\n252\tII-B Fingering Ambiguity and Playability\n253\tSymbolic tablature inference is inherently ambiguous because a single pitch can\n254\toften be played at multiple string–fret positions. For example, in standard\n255\ttuning, A2 can be played either on the sixth string at the fifth fret or on the\n256\topen fifth string. Thus, a MIDI pitch sequence does not uniquely determine a\n257\ttablature sequence.\n258\tClassical approaches address this ambiguity through constrained optimization.\n259\tDynamic programming, genetic algorithms, and probabilistic models have been used\n260\tto search for playable fingering sequences under physical or playability-related\n261\tconstraints [17, 20, 14, 2, 7, 1]. These methods\n262\texplicitly model feasibility and playability, but they often rely on manually\n263\tdesigned cost functions and local transition assumptions.\n264\tIn contrast, Transformer-based models [5] can learn string–fret preferences from\n265\tdata, but they may still produce pitch-invalid or dataset-inconsistent TAB\n266\tpredictions.\n267\tII-C Constrained Decoding for Structured Generation\n268\tConstrained decoding restricts the set of valid output tokens during generation\n269\twithout retraining the model. It has been used in structured generation tasks\n270\tsuch as lexically constrained translation and text-to-SQL generation, where\n271\tinvalid tokens are filtered during decoding [6, 12, 18].\n272\tIn guitar tablature, pitch-validity constraint has also been used as post-generation correction. The Fretting Transformer [5] applies rule-based post-processing techniques, including overlap correction and neighborhood search, to correct generated string–fret combinations that produce incorrect pitches.\n273\tFigure 1: Overview of the proposed framework for guitar tablature transcription. The model takes MIDI-like event tokens as input and generates a token sequence that includes TAB tokens for representing string and fret positions.\n274\tIII Methodology\n275\tWe formulate guitar tablature transcription as a sequence-to-sequence translation problem from MIDI-derived note events to a structured\n276\ttoken sequence.\n277\tAs shown in Figure 1, the raw symbolic data are tokenized into event tokens and converted into token IDs before being fed into the T5 encoder–decoder model. The input token sequence contains NOTE_ON_PITCH, NOTE_OFF_PITCH, and TIME_SHIFT_TICKS tokens.\n278\tTIME_SHIFT_TICKS represents a discrete time interval measured in ticks, where one quarter note equals 960 ticks.\n279\tThe output token sequence specifies both the note-event structure and the physical string–fret realization of each note. The detailed decoder token representation is described below.\n280\tIII-1 Decoder Token Representation\n281\tUnlike the Fretting Transformer, whose decoder vocabulary mainly consists of\n282\tTIME_SHIFT_TICKS and TAB&lt;string,fret&gt; tokens, our\n283\tdecoder also includes NOTE_ON_PITCH and\n284\tNOTE_OFF_PITCH tokens. For each note event, the decoder generates\n285\ta NOTE_ON_PITCH token together with a\n286\tTAB&lt;string,fret&gt; token that specifies its playing position, while\n287\tTIME_SHIFT_TICKS and NOTE_OFF_PITCH tokens encode\n288\ttiming and note-release events.\n289\tBy adding explicit note-event tokens, the output sequence exposes note\n290\tboundaries and pitch-related information during generation. Figure 2\n291\tcompares the proposed decoder token representation with the Fretting Transformer.\n292\tFigure 2: Comparison of the decoder token representations used by the Fretting Transformer and our method.\n293\tIII-2 Architecture\n294\tWe use T5 [13], a Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer, as our backbone model.\n295\tThe encoder consumes the input event tokens and produces contextual representations, and the decoder autoregressively generates the target TAB token sequence.\n296\tOur configuration is: dmodel=128d_{\\text{model}}=128, dff=1024d_{\\text{ff}}=1024, Nenc=3N_{\\text{enc}}=3, Ndec=3N_{\\text{dec}}=3, and the number of heads is h=4h=4. For regularization, we use a weight decay coefficient of 0.10.1 and a dropout rate of 0.10.1.\n297\tIII-3 Training objective\n298\tWe train the model with teacher forcing to minimize the token-level cross-entropy between the predicted sequence and the ground-truth sequence:\n299\tLCE=−∑i=1nlog⁡p​(yi∣y&lt;i,x),L_{\\text{CE}}=-\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\log p\\left(y_{i}\\mid y_{&lt;i},x\\right),\n300\t(1)\n301\twhere xx is the input event-token sequence, yiy_{i} is the ground-truth token at position ii, and p​(⋅)p(\\cdot) is the decoder’s predicted distribution.\n302\tIII-4 Pitch-Validity Constrained Decoding\n303\tThe Fretting Transformer applies a rule-based post-processing step to correct invalid predictions after the complete output token sequence has been generated [5].\n304\tIn contrast, we apply the pitch-validity constraint during decoding. This prevents pitch-invalid TAB&lt;string,fret&gt; tokens from being selected in the autoregressive generation process and simultaneously preserves the original timing and note structure from the input, so that only the string–fret ambiguity remains to be resolved.\n305\tSince the input MIDI-like sequence specifies the pitch of each note event, we use the source pitch at each TAB prediction step to construct a valid set of string–fret candidates. For a target pitch pp, the pitch-valid TAB set is defined as\n306\t𝒯​(p)={(s,f)|tuning​[s]+f=p,s∈[1,6],f∈[0,24]},\\mathcal{T}(p)=\\bigl\\{(s,\\,f)\\;\\big|\\;\\text{tuning}[s]+f=p,\\;s\\!\\in\\![1,6],\\;f\\!\\in\\![0,24]\\bigr\\},\n307\t(2)\n308\twhere tuning​[s]\\text{tuning}[s] is the MIDI pitch of the open string ss, and f=0f=0 denotes an open\n309\tstring. We index strings from the lowest-pitched to the highest-pitched\n310\tstring, so the standard-tuning open-string pitches are\n311\to=[40,45,50,55,59,64]o=[40,45,50,55,59,64], corresponding to E2, A2, D3, G3, B3, and E4.\n312\tAt each decoding step corresponding to a TAB&lt;string,fret&gt; token, all logits associated with string–fret pairs outside 𝒯​(p)\\mathcal{T}(p) are set to −∞-\\infty before token selection.\n313\tBy construction, all generated TAB tokens in this setting are pitch-valid with respect to the source pitch skeleton. Therefore, pitch-validity constrained decoding is used as a diagnostic setting to estimate the performance achievable when pitch-invalid TAB candidates are removed and to analyze the remaining difficulty of selecting the ground-truth string–fret position among multiple pitch-valid candidates.\n314\tIV Experiment\n315\tIV-A Dataset\n316\tWe select 5,185 songs from the DadaGP dataset [16]. For consistency, we retain only a single guitar track per song and restrict the data to 6-string guitar pieces, stored as GuitarPro .gp files. We split the dataset into training, validation, and test sets with 4,148 / 518 / 519 songs, respectively.\n317\tFor the François Leduc dataset [15], we use 79 songs in total, split into training, validation, and test sets with 55 / 16 / 8 songs, respectively. Due to the limited size of the training set, we apply pitch transposition augmentation by shifting each training song by ±\\pm1 to ±\\pm5 semitones. Finally, we get 605 training files (55 songs ×\\times 11 transpositions).\n318\tIV-B Evaluation Metrics\n319\tWe evaluate transcription correctness using three metrics. (i) Token Accuracy measures the token-level exact-match rate between the predicted sequence and the ground-truth target sequence, reflecting overall generation quality. (ii) Pitch Accuracy measures whether the pitch inferred from each TAB token matches the pitch inferred from the corresponding ground-truth TAB token. (iii) Tablature Accuracy measures the match of the predicted string and fret positions to the ground truth at the corresponding TAB tokens.\n320\tFurthermore, We additionally report Difficulty Score [5] based on fretboard movements and fingering transitions as a reference metric for\n321\tplayability, where lower scores indicate more playable tablature.\n322\tIV-C Results\n323\tTables I and II present the evaluation results on the DadaGP and Leduc test sets under different training data settings.\n324\tAll models are trained for 300 epochs in each setting.\n325\tBold indicates the better result within each training setting.\n326\tAs shown in Table I, our method outperforms the Fretting Transformer across all transcription metrics, except for difficulty metric.\n327\tWhen trained only on DadaGP, our method achieves better token, pitch, and tablature accuracy than the Fretting Transformer, with improvements of 10.56%, 4.4%, and 5.32%, respectively.\n328\tThis result suggests that the proposed method helps the model more effectively learn the mapping from MIDI-like event representations to TAB tokens.\n329\tUnder the combined training setting, our method shows a slight improvement across all transcription metrics, whereas the Fretting Transformer shows a small accuracy drop.\n330\tWe further evaluate both methods on the Leduc test set, as shown in Table II. When trained only on Leduc, the Fretting Transformer severely overfits and achieves only 0.49% tablature accuracy, even after hyperparameter tuning.\n331\tIn contrast, our method demonstrates stable training on the\n332\tsmall-scale Leduc dataset and achieves 98.06% tablature\n333\taccuracy.\n334\tUnder the combined training setting, the Fretting Transformer obtains slightly higher accuracy than our method on the Leduc test set.\n335\tNevertheless, considering the limited scale of Leduc, our method remains competitive in the combined setting and demonstrates better stability when trained directly on the small-scale dataset.\n336\tMethod\n337\tToken Acc.↑\\uparrow\n338\tPitch Acc.↑\\uparrow\n339\tTab Acc.↑\\uparrow\n340\tDifficulty ↓\\downarrow\n341\tTest on DadaGP (trained on DadaGP only)\n342\tFretting Transformer\n343\t80.57%\n344\t85.66%\n345\t71.78%\n346\t3.138\n347\tOurs\n348\t91.13%\n349\t90.06%\n350\t77.10%\n351\t3.238\n352\tTest on DadaGP (trained on DadaGP + Leduc)\n353\tFretting Transformer\n354\t80.02%\n355\t84.10%\n356\t71.47%\n357\t3.311\n358\tOurs\n359\t91.72%\n360\t90.51%\n361\t77.61%\n362\t3.315\n363\tTABLE I: Evaluation results on the DadaGP test set under different training data settings.\n364\tMethod\n365\tToken Acc.↑\\uparrow\n366\tPitch Acc.↑\\uparrow\n367\tTab Acc.↑\\uparrow\n368\tDifficulty ↓\\downarrow\n369\tTest on Leduc (trained on Leduc only)\n370\tFretting Transformer\n371\t18.99%\n372\t0.49%\n373\t0.49%\n374\t1.672\n375\tOurs\n376\t97.80%\n377\t98.04%\n378\t98.06%\n379\t2.690\n380\tTest on Leduc (trained on DadaGP + Leduc)\n381\tFretting Transformer\n382\t95.69%\n383\t95.97%\n384\t95.97%\n385\t2.681\n386\tOurs\n387\t95.15%\n388\t95.24%\n389\t95.26%\n390\t2.689\n391\tTABLE II: Evaluation results on the Leduc test set under different training data settings.\n392\tIV-D Error Analysis\n393\t(a) “311 - Loco” from the DadaGP dataset.\n394\t(b) “Paradigm Shift” from the DadaGP dataset.\n395\tFigure 3: \n396\tGuitar tablature comparison between the Fretting Transformer, our method, and the ground truth.\n397\tThe green boxes highlight position-ambiguity cases, where the predicted notes produce the same pitch as the ground truth but use different string–fret positions.\n398\tThe red boxes highlight pitch errors.\n399\tWe first analyze the prediction errors under free decoding, where no pitch-validity constraint is applied. To identify the main sources of error, we perform a note-level decomposition and categorize each prediction outcome into timing, pitch, position, or correct classes.\n400\tWe define the four note-level classes as follows:\n401\t• \n402\tTiming: the predicted note event occurs at an incorrect temporal position.\n403\t• \n404\tPitch: the predicted note is temporally aligned with the ground truth, but its pitch is incorrect.\n405\t• \n406\tPosition: the predicted note has the correct timing and pitch, but its string–fret assignment differs from the ground truth tablature position.\n407\t• \n408\tCorrect: the predicted note matches the ground truth in timing, pitch, and string–fret position.\n409\tTable III reports the performance metrics and the corresponding note-level error decomposition rates on DadaGP, where Tab Acc. corresponds to the correct rate.\n410\tThe results show that our method reduces all three types of errors compared with the Fretting Transformer.\n411\tIn particular, our method produces fewer timing errors, which is consistent with the intended role of explicit NOTE_ON_PITCH events in providing note-level structure to the decoder. It also reduces pitch errors and position errors, indicating that the proposed framework more often predicts both the correct pitch and the ground-truth string–fret position. Overall, the free decoding error decomposition suggests that our method improves tablature prediction by reducing temporal misalignment, pitch mistakes, and pitch-correct but position-mismatched TAB predictions.\n412\tMethod\n413\tConstraint\n414\tPerformance\n415\tError Decomposition\n416\tToken Acc.↑\\uparrow\n417\tTab Acc.↑\\uparrow\n418\tTiming↓\\downarrow\n419\tPitch↓\\downarrow\n420\tPosition↓\\downarrow\n421\tFretting Transformer\n422\tFree\n423\t80.57%\n424\t71.78%\n425\t4.43%\n426\t9.91%\n427\t13.88%\n428\tRule-based [5]\n429\t81.17%\n430\t72.82%\n431\t4.43%\n432\t8.15%\n433\t14.6%\n434\tConstrained decoding\n435\t90.17%\n436\t83.65%\n437\t0.00%\n438\t0.00%\n439\t16.35%\n440\tOurs\n441\tFree\n442\t91.13%\n443\t77.10%\n444\t2.56%\n445\t7.38%\n446\t12.96%\n447\tRule-based [5]\n448\t91.57%\n449\t77.41%\n450\t2.56%\n451\t6.82%\n452\t13.22%\n453\tConstrained decoding\n454\t95.77%\n455\t85.05%\n456\t0.00%\n457\t0.00%\n458\t14.95%\n459\tTABLE III: Performance comparison and error decomposition of the Fretting Transformer and our method under free decoding, rule-based post-processing and pitch-validity constrained decoding on DadaGP. Error decomposition values indicate the percentage of each error type.\n460\tTo further illustrate these error types, we visualize two representative tablature examples in Figure 3. In Figure 3(a), the Fretting Transformer often assigns notes to higher fret positions on lower strings, whereas the ground truth uses open or lower-fret positions on higher strings. These predictions can still produce the correct pitch, but they differ from the ground-truth tablature because of string–fret position ambiguity.\n461\tIn Figure 3(b), the Fretting Transformer also produces pitch errors, highlighted by the red boxes. By contrast, our method predicts the correct pitches in these regions, and its remaining mismatches are mostly pitch-valid string–fret alternatives.\n462\tIV-E Pitch-Validity Constrained Decoding\n463\tThe free-decoding error analysis shows that TAB errors can arise from three different sources: timing mismatch, pitch-invalid predictions and pitch-valid but ground-truth-mismatched string–fret choices. To further separate these three sources of error, we evaluate both methods under the pitch-validity constrained decoding defined in Section III-4.\n464\tWe compare two decoding conditions: free decoding, where the model generates tokens without any validity constraint, and pitch-validity constrained decoding, where a logit mask restricts each TAB prediction to the pitch-valid set 𝒯​(p)\\mathcal{T}(p).\n465\tThis setting removes TAB candidates that cannot physically produce the target pitch and therefore allows us to separate pitch-level errors from pitch-valid string–fret selection errors.\n466\tIn addition, we compare with rule-base post-processing based on the procedure described in [5].\n467\tSince the source code was not released, we reproduce the method according to the paper description.\n468\tEach TAB token is corrected to the nearest playable string–fret position whenever its implied pitch differs from the target pitch specified by the input sequence.\n469\tTable III reports the results under Free decoding, pitch-validity constrained decoding, and rule-based post-processing in the DadaGP-only training setting.\n470\tCompared with free decoding, pitch-validity constrained decoding improves tablature accuracy for both models. The Fretting Transformer improves from 71.78% to 83.65%, a gain\n471\tof 11.87 percentage points, whereas our method improves from 77.10% to 85.05%, a gain of 7.95 percentage points. The larger gain of the Fretting\n472\tTransformer suggests that a greater portion of its free-decoding errors comes from pitch-invalid TAB predictions. In contrast, our method already produces\n473\tfewer pitch-invalid predictions under free decoding, so it benefits less from the constraint but still achieves higher token accuracy and tablature accuracy under pitch-validity constrained decoding.\n474\tWe also observe that the rule-based post-processing improves slightly compare with free decoding. However, the improvement is limited because the rule-based post-processing cannot align timing mismatches.\n475\tThese results extend the previous error analysis. Pitch-validity constrained decoding improves overall tablature accuracy for both models. However, it does not eliminate all TAB errors. After pitch-level mistakes are controlled, the remaining errors are concentrated in cases where the model selects a pitch-valid string–fret position that differs from the ground-truth annotation. Thus, pitch-validity constrained decoding reveals an error-composition trade-off: it improves pitch consistency, but the remaining difficulty shifts toward exact string–fret selection among multiple pitch-valid candidates.\n476\tThis also shows that pitch-valid string–fret ambiguity remains a key challenge even when pitch-invalid predictions are removed.\n477\tV Conclusion\n478\tWe proposed a guitar tablature transcription framework that combines explicit note-event tokenization, regularized training, and pitch-validity constrained decoding.\n479\tBy incorporating NOTE_ON_PITCH and NOTE_OFF_PITCH tokens into the decoder output, the proposed tokenization makes note-event structure more explicit during tablature generation.\n480\tExperiments on the DadaGP and François Leduc datasets show that our framework improves pitch and tablature accuracy over the retrained Fretting Transformer baseline, particularly under the small-scale Leduc training setting.\n481\tOur note-level error analysis and pitch-validity constrained decoding experiments show that removing pitch-invalid TAB candidates improves tablature accuracy for both models, but exact string–fret selection remains challenging even when pitch-level errors are controlled. In future work, we plan to incorporate fingering information and playability-aware inference to better model performer-preferred tablature choices.\n482\tReferences\n483\t[1]\n484\tS. Ariga, S. Fukayama, and M. Goto (2017)\n485\tSong2Guitar: a difficulty-aware arrangement system for generating guitar solo covers from polyphonic audio of popular music.\n486\tIn International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference,\n487\tExternal Links: Link\n488\tCited by: §II-B.\n489\t[2]\n490\tI. Barbancho, L. J. Tardón, and A. M. Barbancho (2009)\n491\tAutomatic transcription of guitar chords and fingering from audio.\n492\tIEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing.\n493\tCited by: §II-B.\n494\t[3]\n495\tY. Chen, Y. Huang, W. Hsiao, and Y. Yang (2020)\n496\tAutomatic composition of guitar tabs by transformers and groove modeling.\n497\tInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval.\n498\tCited by: §II-A.\n499\t[4]\n500\tD. Edwards, X. Riley, P. Sarmento, and S. Dixon (2024)\n501\tMIDI-to-tab: guitar tablature inference via masked language modeling.\n502\tExternal Links: 2408.05024,\n503\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "1f5653b7-6f30-440d-a61f-3d114028f62f", "uuid": "ca546406-6964-43fd-9f81-65583cc5a6aa", "timestamp": "2026-07-30T06:02:04.855Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/1f5653b7-6f30-440d-a61f-3d114028f62f/tool-results/b3jz2y7r0.txt", "content": "142428\n---\nExplicit Note-Event Tokenization and Pitch-Validity Constrained Decoding for MIDI-to-Tablature Transcription\n  // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or\n  // enact the browser preference if \"automatic\", \n  // without expecting DOM load to have completed.\n  //\n  // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences.\n  function initializeReadingPreferences() {\n    let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem(\"ar5iv_theme\") || \"automatic\";\n    if (saved_theme === \"automatic\") {\n      if (window.matchMedia(\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\").matches) {\n        saved_theme = \"dark\";\n      }\n    }\n    if (saved_theme == \"dark\") {\n      document.documentElement.setAttribute(\"data-theme\", \"dark\");\n    } else {\n      document.documentElement.setAttribute(\"data-theme\", \"light\");\n    }\n    const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display');\n    if (tocDisplay) {\n      document.documentElement.setAttribute(\"data-toc-display\", tocDisplay);\n    }\n    const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode');\n    if (readingMode) {\n      document.documentElement.setAttribute(\"data-reading-mode\", readingMode);\n    }\n    // Pre-apply spinout-banner